36. MANDATORY UNIVERSAL FIXES — LESSONS FROM THE WORKING STUDENT FOLLOW-UP REACT BUILD
PRIORITY: This section is a binding clarification of sections 4–8, 16–21, 29–35 for EVERY future Prudle React conversion. It is application-independent. Never copy the Student Follow-up UI, collections, filenames, or data logic into another app. The goal is to prevent a technically successful React build from failing to start after deployment.

36.1 ESTABLISH A WORKING BASELINE BEFORE REACT CONVERSION
- Inspect the complete supplied original application and the actual working deployment example, if provided.
- Distinguish the original HTML application, the compiled React build, the host page, and the deployed Worker. Do not assume that a problem in one is caused by another.
- Inventory exact runtime session keys, original login/logout behavior, Worker requests, UID mapping, timestamp formats, domain/origin assumptions, script order, and asset paths.
- Record whether the app is hosted directly at GitHub Pages, inside an iframe, or mounted through JS/CSS tags in Webflow; these are distinct environments.
- Do not infer success merely from the original page loading or from npm run build passing.

36.2 SESSION TIMESTAMP NORMALIZATION — NO FALSE LOGOUTS
- Read the approved session-start value from the actual storage keys used by the existing login system; inspect BOTH localStorage and sessionStorage only where the original architecture already uses them.
- Recognize, when present in the approved original system: integer millisecond Unix timestamps, integer second Unix timestamps, numeric strings, ISO-8601 strings, and documented legacy date formats.
- Normalize valid timestamps to epoch milliseconds before computing the session deadline. Reject impossible dates, NaN, nonfinite values, and future timestamps beyond reasonable clock skew.
- Use a single shared parser in the auth service; never scatter contradictory Date.parse(), Number(), or new Date() assumptions through components.
- If the approved system has an absolute 24-hour session, calculate expiresAt = originalAuthenticatedSessionStart + 24*60*60*1000. Page reloads, React remounts, token refreshes, or new tabs MUST NOT reset that start time.
- Do not use legacy loginDate !== today as a new logout requirement when the approved system uses a 24-hour deadline. Preserve loginDate as metadata if needed.
- If no trustworthy start time exists, do NOT create a fresh 24-hour window. Use only a verified backend session-restoration/validation flow, otherwise treat the session as unrecoverable and redirect to login.
- A timestamp stored in the browser is not an authoritative security guarantee; server-side absolute expiry must be independently verified. If unverified, report SERVER-SIDE 24-HOUR ENFORCEMENT NOT VERIFIED.
- A short-lived Firebase ID token expiry is different from the absolute Prudle session expiry. Refresh only through an existing, verified flow; never invent a route or persist a refresh token for convenience.
- On transient network failure, preserve valid credentials and show only a safe bounded connection/retry state. On confirmed invalid/revoked session, clear only app-owned credentials and redirect.

36.3 STRICT HOST ALLOWLIST — PRODUCTION VS DEVELOPMENT
- Derive the approved production hostnames from the actual app configuration. Current common production examples are prudle-education.com, www.prudle-education.com, and abbassaboali-alt.github.io; inclusion must still be authorized for the specific app.
- Require HTTPS for production hosts. Do not use permissive substring checks such as hostname.includes('github'), hostname.includes('webcontainer'), or hostname.endsWith('.run.app').
- Development bypass, when explicitly needed, must be limited to exact localhost or 127.0.0.1 with a documented dev-only mode, and must never be treated as proof of user authorization.
- For unauthorized production hosts, prevent protected app initialization and use the approved redirect. Never use host checks as a substitute for Worker-side UID and role authorization.
- Verify the origin of postMessage-based authentication before accepting data. GitHub Pages and Webflow do not share localStorage or sessionStorage across origins.

36.4 CORRECT INDEX.HTML, ASSET PATHS, AND DEPLOYMENT LAYOUT
- Build the app with Vite and verify the actual generated output; do not hand-invent hashed filenames.
- Deliver the deployable folder with index.html at its root and all referenced assets under assets/. Keep the filenames exactly as emitted by that same build.
- For portable, self-contained deployment where applicable, configure Vite base: './' and confirm that index.html uses relative ./assets/... references. For a fixed repository path, use its verified correct base instead. The base must match the real deployment mode.
- If embedding the compiled JS/CSS directly into a Webflow page, use absolute URLs pointing to the same deployed build and the exact current hashed filenames. Ensure the module script, stylesheet, and root mount are all present in the Webflow page.
- Distinguish loading an HTML page in an iframe from injecting its assets into Webflow: relative imports, root element, CSS cascade, storage origin, and auth handoff differ.
- Preserve Vite-generated modulepreload and chunk imports; do not rename assets independently of index.html or rewrite import paths blindly.
- Test both a fresh browser session and a hard refresh to detect old cached index.html referencing deleted hashed assets. Use coherent deployment/caching so index and assets come from the same build.
- Do not deploy a ZIP archive as a single file. Extract its contents so index.html and assets/ are actual deployed files.

36.5 PRE-RENDER AUTH GATE WITHOUT AUTHENTICATION REGRESSION
- Keep protected React UI unmounted and hidden until the approved authentication flow and backend authorization finish.
- If credentials are definitely missing, redirect directly to the approved login URL with no intermediate app/login screen.
- If credentials are present, attempt only the approved validation and recovery flow; do not force a redirect merely because one legacy timestamp parser fails before checking supported formats.
- Confirm authenticated UID through the verified token/Worker, then verify the app's own user record and role. Never treat a client-stored UID or role as sufficient authorization.
- Do not add new authentication routes, browser Firebase configuration, or a new logout button where none existed.
- Prevent StrictMode/effect replay from starting duplicate initialization, refreshes, listeners, or writes.
- Differentiate confirmed 401 invalidity, 403 permission denial, temporary connectivity errors, and unavailable Worker; do not create infinite redirects or spinners.

36.6 REACT CONVERSION MUST PRESERVE ACTUAL APPLICATION OPERATIONS
- Map every original function, event, timer, query, mutation, field name, calculation, PDF/export action, and permission rule to the React equivalent.
- Keep the existing Worker payload/response contract and its exact authentication headers. Do not invent /me, /auth/refresh, or unsupported database actions.
- Preserve polling intervals and unsubscribe behavior as implemented in the original application; do not impose a universal 12-second or 30-second interval.
- Use AbortController or equivalent cancellation where appropriate and ensure stale requests cannot overwrite a new user's data.
- Ensure database writes are confirmed by the backend before reporting success. Never change partial updates to full overwrites or atomic writes to non-atomic fallbacks without approval.
- Keep the original CSS and UI structure as closely as React permits; avoid introducing global resets that change original visual behavior.

36.7 OBFUSCATION COMPATIBILITY IS A FUNCTIONAL RELEASE GATE
